WEBVTT

00:03.200 --> 00:06.031
Small teams post the same job on four boards,

00:06.051 --> 00:08.482
and get a pile of applications back.

00:09.000 --> 00:11.994
But they never find out which listing brought the person they hired.

00:13.500 --> 00:17.726
recruited.tools is an open-source applicant tracker that answers that.

00:17.746 --> 00:20.699
The demo runs on sample data from a music agency.

00:20.719 --> 00:23.356
Three roles, seven listings, four boards.

00:23.900 --> 00:26.438
The home screen doesn't lead with applications.

00:26.458 --> 00:29.002
It leads with what an A-tier candidate cost.

00:30.600 --> 00:34.647
Same board, same spend. Variant A got twice the applications.

00:34.667 --> 00:38.248
Variant B paid more, and named the tools in the title.

00:38.268 --> 00:41.752
It brought 3.7× more A-tier candidates per dollar.

00:43.600 --> 00:45.699
Every variant gets its own apply page,

00:45.719 --> 00:48.824
so each application knows which listing it came from.

00:49.900 --> 00:52.895
Every applicant, from every board, lands on one pipeline,

00:52.915 --> 00:55.286
tagged with the variant that brought them.

00:57.300 --> 01:00.272
Interviewers score on their own, and the summary is drawn only

01:00.292 --> 01:03.000
from what the candidate sent and what the team wrote.

01:04.300 --> 01:07.775
Posting a role means writing two variants and picking the boards.

01:07.795 --> 01:09.774
The test starts when it goes live.

01:11.800 --> 01:14.532
It's open source, and it deploys in five minutes.

01:14.552 --> 01:17.622
Hire from the listing that brings A-tier candidates.
